                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>That's the sustainable-careers read.</strong> You placed the mid-career breakage at the organisation and life-stage level and the senior breakage at the labour-market and organisation level — then framed the whole thing across the life-course. Now your interventions can target where each problem actually lives, instead of throwing training at all three.`,
                    coach_r1_midskills:     `<strong>“Under-skilled — more training will fix it” is the universal-remedy reflex.</strong> The mid-career group isn't short of skills; they're plateaued, last developed four years ago, and squeezed by caregiving. That's an organisation and life-stage problem. More courses won't fix a job-design and pathing failure.`,
                    coach_r1_midpay:        `<strong>It isn't a pay problem.</strong> You already win awards for pay and perks — and they're still leaving at 24%. What the mid-career group lacks is a <em>path</em> and life-stage flexibility, not another comp band. Diagnose the interface, not the symptom.`,
                    coach_r1_seniorattitude:`<strong>“They've stopped trying” blames the employee for an organisation problem.</strong> The senior specialists have deep but obsolescing skills and low external employability — that erodes meaning and engagement. The breakage lives at the labour-market and organisation interface, not in their attitude.`,
