                    coach_r2_earlymore:     `<strong>Doubling down on the high-potentials is tunnel vision in intervention form.</strong> Early-career are already the best-served third; piling on more elite training while they burn out adds load, not sustainability — and keeps the budget pointed at the group that was already fine.`,
                    coach_r2_earlyignore:   `<strong>“Leave them be” lets the burnout run.</strong> Early-career are well-developed but burning out on always-on loads; doing nothing about job design lets their wellbeing — and eventually their retention — slide. Sustainability is for all three profiles, including the favoured one.`,
                    coach_r2_midtraining:   `<strong>You answered the plateau with a course catalogue.</strong> That's training-as-a-universal-remedy. The mid-career breakage is organisational and life-stage — a path and caregiving flexibility — so more classroom training can't reach it. Which interface level does this problem actually live at?`,
                    coach_r2_midperk:       `<strong>A wellness app for a pathing problem is perks over paths.</strong> The mid-career group doesn't want a meditation app; they want a career that's going somewhere and life-stage flexibility. A perk treats the symptom while the plateau — and the 24% turnover — stays.`,
                    coach_r2_seniorhoard:   `<strong>Hoarding employability is the paradox you're failing.</strong> Withholding market-recognised skills for fear they'll quit leaves your senior specialists with obsolescing skills and the lowest wellbeing in the firm — disengaging in place. De Vos's evidence: visible employability investment, paired with meaningful roles, often <em>raises</em> retention.`,
                    coach_r2_seniorperk:    `<strong>A sabbatical isn't a career.</strong> Recognition perks make senior staff feel briefly valued, but they don't renew the obsolescing skills that erode their employability and meaning. Wellbeing is one leg of the triad — where are productivity and happiness?`,
                    coach_r2_unbalanced:    `<strong>You maximised one leg and starved the others.</strong> Push everything toward wellbeing (or everything toward employability) and the triad breaks — a stretch assignment with no guardrail burns people out, and comfort with no growth disengages them. Health, happiness and productivity are jointly necessary.`,
                    coach_r3_lifecourse:    `<strong>That's a life-course allocation done as a redesign.</strong> You rebalanced away from early-career's 78%, funded both neglected groups with a real share, and gave early-career a redesigned value proposition instead of just cutting them. Rebalancing isn't robbing Peter to pay Paul — it's making all three profiles sustainable.`,
                    coach_r3_tunnel:        `<strong>You kept the budget on early-career — the headline error.</strong> The ROI looks cleanest, but mid-career turnover is still 24%, €8M a year is leaking out of your client-holding staff, and the works council just filed its grievance. You optimised the group that was already fine. Sustainability spans the whole life-course — so why does your budget still serve one third of it?`,
                    coach_r3_overcorrect:   `<strong>You swung too far the other way.</strong> Starving early-career below a fifth of the budget creates a brand-new neglected segment — the burnout you didn't fund will surface as its own flight risk. Rebalancing is about making <em>all three</em> sustainable, not flipping which third is starved.`,
                    coach_r3_seniorstarved: `<strong>The senior third is still under-funded.</strong> They carry the lowest employability and wellbeing and the highest obsolescence — leaving them with a token share is exactly the neglect the works council is escalating about. Fund the employability-plus-meaning intervention you designed.`,
                    coach_r3_zerosum:       `<strong>You cut early-career and offered them nothing.</strong> That's a zero-sum raid, not a redesign. Your high-potentials will read the shrinking training budget as a downgrade and start walking. Rebalancing has to come with a redesigned value proposition — stretch roles, mentoring, guardrails — for the group you moved spend away from.`,
                    coach_r3_wrongrisk:     `<strong>Early-career perks aren't the biggest under-funded risk.</strong> The largest sustainability and grievance risk is the neglected senior employability and mid-career pathing — the very groups the works council is escalating about. Naming the wrong risk means you'll defend the wrong line.`,
                    coach_r4_board:         `<strong>That's the board-ready recovery.</strong> You answered the poaching with path-and-meaning instead of killing the upskilling, moved money to what was improving sustainability, and built a standing system so it outlasts the cycle. Four KPIs moving together is the case the board funds again.`,
                    coach_r4_stoptraining:  `<strong>Stopping the upskilling is hoarding employability under pressure.</strong> Yes, the competitor is poaching the cohort you just made more marketable — but pulling their development re-traps them in obsolescence and tells everyone investment is conditional. Pair employability with meaningful internal roles instead; make staying the better choice.`,
                    coach_r4_counteroffer:  `<strong>Reactive counter-offers are recruitment, not sustainability.</strong> Buying people back at the door is the €8M leak by another name — it doesn't renew careers and it teaches staff that the way to a raise is to resign. The budget was meant to make careers last, not to bid against poachers.`,
                    coach_r4_cutworking:    `<strong>You cut what was working to rescue what wasn't.</strong> Mid-career mobility was reducing turnover; defunding it to pour money into a senior program with a credibility problem loses on both ends. Fund what's improving sustainability and fix the senior program's <em>design</em> — uptake is low because they doubt their market value, not because it's underfunded.`,
                    coach_r4_hold:          `<strong>Holding every line ignores the mid-cycle evidence.</strong> Some interventions are landing and one isn't; refusing to reallocate isn't decisiveness, it's wasting the signal. Sustainability work is iterative — move money to what's working and fix or cut what isn't.`,
                    coach_r4_survey:        `<strong>A survey measures the problem; it doesn't build the system.</strong> Continuous monitoring is part of it, but without life-stage career conversations and wellbeing guardrails as standing practice, the gains evaporate the moment this budget cycle ends.`,
                    coach_r4_nosystem:      `<strong>“A one-off budget exercise” guarantees the debt returns.</strong> Without a standing system — life-stage career conversations, continuous-employability monitoring, wellbeing guardrails — next year's review will find the same crisis. Sustainability has to outlast the cycle that funded it.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
