                    insight_sustainable:    `Careers made sustainable: you ran the genuine life-course pattern — each breakage matched to its interface level, the €4.2M rebalanced as a redesign rather than a raid, employability and wellbeing built to reinforce, and a standing system to outlast the cycle. The index rose well above your baseline, retention recovered, and the board has its evidence.`,
                    insight_tunnel:         `High-potential tunnel vision: you kept the development budget pointed at the early-career third that was already well-served. Short-term ROI looked clean while mid-career flight and senior obsolescence compounded — exactly the sustainability debt De Vos warns the whole-life-course lens exists to prevent. The works-council grievance was the bill coming due.`,
                    insight_hoarding:       `Employability hoarded: you withheld market-recognised investment from senior specialists for fear it would help them quit — and left them disengaging in place with obsolescing skills, which is worse than attrition. The paradox held: visible employability investment paired with meaning would have raised retention, not lowered it.`,
                    insight_training:       `Training as a cure-all: you answered organisation- and life-stage-level breakages with individual training that can't reach them. The mid-career plateau needed a path and caregiving flexibility, not a course catalogue — one remedy for three different problems leaves the real breakages untouched.`,
                    insight_zerosum:        `Zero-sum rebalance: you bravely cut early-career to fund the neglected groups but offered the cut group nothing in return. Rebalancing without a redesigned value proposition reads as a downgrade, and the high-potentials you stopped funding started walking. Rebalancing must be a redesign, not a raid.`,
                    insight_perks:          `Perks over paths: you maximised wellbeing benefits while leaving employability and meaning thin, so engagement kept slipping. A meditation app and a sabbatical aren't a career that's going somewhere — health is one leg of the triad, and happiness and productivity were missing from the plan.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed cycle: some interventions matched their interface level and some didn't, so the four KPIs moved unevenly. Enough sustainability debt remains in the neglected profiles for the board to question whether the gains will hold past this budget cycle.`,
                    insight_csi_flat:       `The Career Sustainability Index barely moved above your baseline: the triad of health, happiness and productivity never improved enough across the whole life-course for the board to call the cycle a success.`,
                    insight_employ_low:     `Employability stayed low: the senior specialists' obsolescing skills were never renewed against the labour market, so a third of the workforce remains at structural sustainability risk.`,
                    insight_wellbeing_low:  `Wellbeing stayed low: the always-on loads and the obsolescence that drive sickness absence were never addressed with job redesign and guardrails — a leading indicator of unsustainable careers left flashing.`,
                    insight_grievance:      `Works-council grievance risk stayed high: the neglected mid-career and senior groups were never funded enough to defuse the escalation, and the budget is now exposed to reverting to reactive hiring.`,
                    insight_saving:         `The retention recovery converts into a real business case: fewer departures at €55,000 each means an estimated annual turnover saving — the number that justifies the development budget against the €8.3M leak.`,
                    insight_saving_neg:     `Retention slipped below the starting 83%: more departures at €55,000 each widen the €8.3M leak rather than closing it — the opposite of the business case the budget had to make.`,

                    hint_tunnel:            `You're running high-potential tunnel vision — the budget still serves the early-career third while the neglected groups break. Rebalance toward mid-career and senior, and give early-career a redesign, not just a cut.`,
                    hint_hoarding:          `You're hoarding employability — withholding market skills leaves senior staff disengaging in place. Fund reskilling paired with meaningful internal roles; visible investment plus meaning raises retention.`,
                    hint_training:          `You're treating different breakages with the same remedy. Match each intervention to its interface level — a plateau needs a path, not another course.`,
                    hint_grievance:         `Works-council grievance risk is dangerously high — the neglected groups are escalating. Fund mid-career pathing and senior employability before the budget reverts to reactive hiring.`,
                    hint_zerosum:           `You rebalanced but offered the cut group nothing — that's a zero-sum raid. Redesign early-career's value proposition (stretch roles, mentoring, guardrails) so it isn't simply robbing Peter to pay Paul.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — the index is well above baseline and employability and wellbeing are healthy. Land it: answer the poaching with path-and-meaning and institutionalise a standing system so it outlasts the cycle.`,

